# browser/CHANGELOG

## Capabilities, and when each arrived

| capability | version | date |
|---|---|---|
| The six browsers named, with their data locations: Brave, Chrome, Chromium, Firefox, Safari, Tor Browser | 0.0.0 | 2022-06-22 |
| Bookmarks and history locations as methods on each browser | 0.1.0 | 2022-12-03 |
| One class per file; top-level names for a program that wants `Chrome` rather than `Browser::Chrome` | 0.3.0 | 2023-01-02 |
| History read from SQLite, rendered to CSV, written to a dated file | 0.4.0 | 2023-01-02 |
| A profile name selects the locations | 0.5.0 | 2023-09-23 |
| The profile name appears in the dumped filename | 0.5.2 | 2023-09-24 |
| Bookmarks read: JSON for the Chromium family, plist for Safari, SQLite for the Firefox family | 0.6.1 | 2026-08-22 |
| Four renderers for both bookmarks and history: CSV, JSON, plist, objects; `dump` takes a format | 0.6.1 | 2026-08-22 |
| Safari's binary plist read natively, via CFPropertyList | 0.6.1 | 2026-08-22 |
| MIT licence, and a LICENSE file | 0.6.1 | 2026-08-22 |
| `Browser.new(:name)`, any case, string or symbol; `browser-classes` for the top-level names | 0.7.0 | 2026-08-23 |
| `dump(format:, filename:, path:)` returns the path written and writes nothing if the render fails | 0.7.0 | 2026-08-23 |
| `readable?`, and `Browser::Unreadable` for data that is absent or locked by a running browser | 0.8.0 | 2026-08-23 |
| A CHANGELOG | 0.9.0 | 2026-08-23 |
| `Browser::Bookmark`, a tree; folders carried; `to_objects` flattens to title, url, folder | 0.10.0 | 2026-08-25 |
| Installable as a gem, standing alone without a RUBYLIB | 0.10.1 | 2026-09-04 |
| A README | 0.11.0 | 2026-09-07 |
| One set of renderers, shared by bookmarks and history | 0.12.0 | 2026-09-09 |
| The Firefox family's default profile, the one profiles.ini names | 0.13.0 | 2026-09-09 |
| Firefox and Tor Browser bookmarks with their folders | 0.14.0 | 2026-09-09 |
| `Browser::Bookmarks#tree`, the bookmarks as a tree, folders and all | 0.17.0 | 2026-10-03 |
| `profile_path`, the directory a profile sits in | 0.17.0 | 2026-10-03 |
| `Browser::Visit`, history as one record whatever the browser, the three epochs converted to a `Time` | 0.18.0 | 2026-10-05 |
| `Browser::History#visits`, the records, each carrying the browser's own columns besides | 0.18.0 | 2026-10-05 |
| Every meaningful history column carried, each browser's own bookkeeping left out | 0.19.0 | 2026-10-06 |
| Safari's history titles, which its query had never asked for | 0.19.0 | 2026-10-06 |


## Capabilities, per browser, and when each arrived

The README's table, with each cell holding the version at which it became true.

| browser | bookmarks | history | folders | profile named | profile default |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Brave | 0.6.1 | 0.4.0 | 0.10.0 | 0.5.0 | 0.0.0 |
| Chrome | 0.6.1 | 0.4.0 | 0.10.0 | 0.5.0 | 0.0.0 |
| Chromium | 0.6.1 | 0.4.0 | 0.10.0 | 0.5.0 | 0.0.0 |
| Firefox | 0.7.0 | 0.7.0 | 0.14.0 | 0.5.0 | 0.13.0 |
| Safari | 0.6.1 | 0.4.0 | 0.10.0 | none | none |
| Tor Browser | 0.7.0 | 0.7.0 | 0.14.0 | 0.7.0 | 0.13.0 |

## 20261006

0.19.0: ~ the history queries: carry everything meaningful, the bookkeeping left out; + Safari's titles.

1. ~ Browser::Safari.history_sql: + history_visits.title, which Safari has held all along and the query had never asked for.
2. So Browser::Visit#title is no longer nil for Safari, and to_objects, to_csv, to_json and to_plist carry it.
3. 0.18.0 shipped the claim that Safari stores no title, in Browser::Visit's header and in README.md, and it was never so.
4. ~ the same: + load_successful, http_non_get, synthesized, redirect_source, redirect_destination, origin, visit_count and status_code.
5. ~ the same: history_visits.id in place of history_items.id, a row being a visit rather than an item.
6. ~ the same: the join driven from history_visits, ON rather than a WHERE, so that a visit whose item is gone arrives with no url in place of vanishing.
7. ~ Browser::FirefoxBased.history_sql: + from_visit, visit_type, session, source, visit_count, hidden, typed, last_visit_date, guid, description, preview_image_url and site_name, four columns of twenty-seven becoming sixteen.
8. - from both: the internal keys, the index helpers, the opaque blobs, the ranking scores and their recalculation flags, which are each browser's own bookkeeping and tell a consumer nothing.
9. Browser::ChromiumBased.history_sql is unchanged, SELECT * over urls carrying all seven of its columns, every one of them meaningful.
10. to_objects is untouched throughout, so the renderers carry the same three fields and the detail rides on attributes.
11. ~ Browser::Visit: the header, which had said Chromium's visit_count, typed_count and hidden have no counterpart in the others; moz_places holds visit_count, hidden and typed, and the query had not asked.
12. ~ Rakefile: + status_code to the Safari fixture's history_items, which the real table has and the fixture lacked.
13. ~ the same: the Firefox fixture's place carrying a visit_count, typed, last_visit_date, guid, description and site_name, and its visit a session and a source.
14. ~ test/fixtures/Safari_history.sqlite, FirefoxBased_places.sqlite rebuilt.
15. ~ test/Browser/Safari_test.rb: the history expected with its titles; + the carried detail and the bookkeeping left out.
16. ~ test/Browser/Firefox_test.rb, TorBrowser_test.rb: the widened sql; + the same.
17. ~ test/Browser/Visit_test.rb: a nil title is a browser which gave none, not Safari.
18. ~ README.md: what attributes carries, in place of the claim about Safari's titles.
19. ~ Browser::VERSION: /0.18.0/0.19.0/


## 20261005

0.18.0: + Browser::Visit, history as one representation whatever the browser.

1. + Browser::Visit: url, title and visited_at, the three every browser holds, with the browser's own row kept as attributes.
2. + Browser::History#visits: the history as those records.
3. ~ Browser::History#to_objects: the three fields, in place of each browser's own columns, which it had zipped from the query.
4. The renderers follow to_objects, so to_csv, to_json and to_plist now carry one shape whatever the browser, which is what converting one browser's history to another's wants; a consumer reading the old columns reads them from attributes now.
5. ~ Browser::History: - column_names taken from the query; + the three.
6. + Browser::ChromiumBased::HISTORY_EPOCH, FirefoxBased's and Safari's: 1601, 1970 and 2001.
7. + .visited_at to each of the three: the epoch plus the stored value, through Rational where that value is microseconds, no float being exact over it.
8. Safari's title is nil throughout, Safari storing none with its history.
9. A Chromium row stays a url and the last visit to it where Firefox's and Safari's are single visits; TODO holds the visits table which would even it.
10. + lib/Browser/Visit.rb, + test/Browser/Visit_test.rb
11. ~ test/Browser/Brave_test.rb, Chrome_test.rb, Chromium_test.rb, Firefox_test.rb, Safari_test.rb, TorBrowser_test.rb: the history expected as the three fields.
12. ~ the same: to_objects compared, where to_json had been, as the bookmarks are.
13. + test/Browser/Chromium_test.rb, Firefox_test.rb, Safari_test.rb: #visits, one per family, for the epoch and the surplus.
14. ~ README.md: + visits and the three epochs; + a Chromium row being a url rather than a visit; ~ the description, bookmarks and history both one representation now.
15. ~ ROADMAP.md: the representation theme, its history half answered.
16. ~ TODO: - the history representation, done; + Chromium's visits table.
17. ~ Browser::VERSION: /0.17.0/0.18.0/


## 20261003

0.17.0: + Browser::Bookmarks#tree, the bookmark tree; + Browser::Base#profile_path, the profile directory.

1. + Browser::Bookmarks#tree: the root Browser::Bookmark, which results holds and nothing outside could reach.
2. to_objects flattens the tree to its leaves, so no folder was readable anywhere.
3. Browser::Bookmark#name goes with them, unreachable since it arrived at 0.14.0 for writing back into a browser.
4. + Browser::Base.profile_path(profile_name:): profiles_path joined to the profile name.
5. + Browser::Base#profile_path: the same for an instance, holding nothing, profile_name having memoised the lookup.
6. Safari needs no override of its own: its profiles_path is nil, and a nil profiles_path is a nil profile_path.
7. A family implementing neither root_path nor profiles_path raises NotImplementedError through the guard it already carries.
8. + test/Browser/Brave_test.rb, Chrome_test.rb, Chromium_test.rb, Firefox_test.rb, Safari_test.rb, TorBrowser_test.rb: .profile_path and #profile_path.
9. + test/Browser/Chromium_test.rb, Safari_test.rb, Firefox_test.rb: #tree, one test per format read.
10. ~ test/Browser/Firefox_test.rb: + #name reached through the tree, Firefox's roots being the titles which differ from their names.
11. ~ browser.rb.gemspec: the description cut to one sentence; the rest restated the README.
12. ~ README.md: + tree and profile_path.
13. ~ ROADMAP.md: driving a browser, with the profile path no longer in the way.
14. ~ ROADMAP.md: + the require path.
15. ~ TODO: - the bookmark tree and the profile directory, both done.
16. ~ TODO: + the gemspec's dependencies derived from lib's requires.
17. ~ CHANGELOG: - How this history is kept.
18. ~ Browser::VERSION: /0.16.0/0.17.0/


## 20260925

0.16.0: ~ Browser::Rendering::CSV: Use CSV from the standard library; ~ Browser::Bookmarks.from_sqlite: Build the Bookmark object directly; ~ ROADMAP.md and TODO tidied.

1. ~ Browser::Rendering::CSV#render: ::CSV.generate, the column names pushed and then each row, in place of joining Array#to_csv_row's.
2. The escaping is no longer ours to get wrong, and the library quotes the fields which need it rather than all of them, so an ordinary row is now unquoted.
3. Taken from the side branch at the tag history-formatter-20250923, whose History::Formatter::CSV rendered this way: its four lines, the results pair become records and column names.
4. + browser.rb.gemspec: csv, which Rendering now requires and which is no default gem from Ruby 3.4.
5. Found by installing the built gem into an empty GEM_HOME.
6. - lib/Array/to_csv_row.rb, - lib/String/wrap.rb, which nothing requires now.
7. With String#ascii_only gone at 0.15.0 the gem ships no monkeypatch at all, and lib/Array and lib/String go with them.
8. ~ test/Browser/Rendering_test.rb: the renderings expected unquoted where a field needs no quoting.
9. The three cases 0.15.0 added each still render and read back as they were.
10. ~ test/loading_test.rb: that the library loads nothing from lib/Array or lib/String, in place of naming a helper which resolves from lib.
11. ~ test/gemspec_test.rb: csv among the runtime dependencies.
12. ~ ROADMAP.md: - whether a CSV library should do the escaping, which this answers.
13. ~ TODO: - the monkeypatches, done; ~ the renderer, to be revisited if a better writer arrives.
14. ~ CHANGELOG: How this history is kept, cut to the two rules.
15. ~ the same: the capabilities table, one version and one date a row, the rows which held several split.
16. ~ the same: - the note on cells later than the entries suggest, which 0.7.0's items already carry.
17. ~ TODO: + readers for the bookmark tree and the profile directory; a history representation; the bookmark roots, dates, guid and separators; Tor Browser's bundle; a Browser::Error for Unreadable; the orphaned SQLite bookmark.
18. ~ Browser::Bookmarks.from_sqlite: builds the root itself, as .from_json and .from_plist do; - sqlite_tree, its only caller.
19. ~ ROADMAP.md: themes of work, in place of open questions.
20. ~ the same: + cookies, a client for scraping, driving a browser; - Tor Browser's install; tags and the history record folded into the representation.
21. ~ Browser::VERSION: /0.15.0/0.16.0/


0.15.0: ~ Array#to_csv_row: a quote within a field doubled, and nothing stripped.

1. ~ Array#to_csv_row: a quote within a field doubled, as RFC 4180 carries one.
2. It had wrapped each field in quotes and left a quote inside it alone, so a title holding one rendered a row no reader could parse.
3. Present since the method arrived at 0.4.0, in History's CSV from then and in Bookmarks' from 0.12.0.
4. No test had ever rendered a field with a quote in it.
5. ~ the same: - String#ascii_only, which had stripped every byte outside printable ASCII.
6. So Café rendered as Caf, and a title in Japanese as an empty field.
7. Quoting every field is what makes a comma or a newline safe; nothing was gained by dropping the rest.
8. - lib/String/ascii_only.rb, which nothing requires now, as lib/String/pascalcase.rb went when its last caller did.
9. It shipped, and monkeypatched String for anyone who loaded the gem.
10. + test/Browser/Rendering_test.rb: a quote, a comma and a newline within a field, and a title which is not ASCII.
11. Each rendered and then read back with the stdlib CSV; all three fail against the old method.
12. ~ test/loading_test.rb: String/wrap in place of String/ascii_only as the helper it checks resolves from lib.
13. + ROADMAP.md: whether a CSV library should do the escaping.
14. ~ TODO: ~ the monkeypatches, two now; + ROADMAP.md on the escaping.
15. ~ Browser::VERSION: /0.14.6/0.15.0/


